The sticking piano key reproduced the low tone. The scenery flat reproduced the scrape when its crooked caster dragged across the stage. The knitted hat and the unreconciled rehearsal sweep provided a recent-use path for the lowered seat. None explained who or what moved the piano key or scenery flat during the gap before entry. No continuous camera covered the auditorium.

Then Abby placed a still from the group’s entrance beside the stage-left display case. The program title and Marian Bell’s name remained legible from the center aisle. Miriam’s writer-or-director impression followed. Her M-R approximation followed that. Evan’s first search produced unrelated people. “Playwright” narrowed the results to another Marian Bell, a regional writer whose work had opened at the Grand Vaudeville Theatre in another state. Evan drew the vaudeville term, portrait, and red-seat image from the historical page. No record connected the writer to Bellweather High or to the local director named on the program.

Evan studied the full chain.

“You can make any discovery look contaminated when you draw enough arrows.”

Abby removed every arrow and played the sources in real time.

The visible program appeared first.

Miriam’s theater impression followed.

Evan supplied “director.”

Miriam offered “writer.”

The group found the program.

Miriam approximated the visible name.

Evan searched that name with “theater,” rejected several matches, added “playwright,” and opened the historical page.

He compared the portrait, theater, and red seats to the auditorium.

Miriam reported a peaceful woman who wished to remain.

Evan told the camera that the cleansing had removed dangerous activity while leaving a protective theater spirit.

Diane stopped playback.

“The sources establish the same order without the arrows,” Abby said.

Evan stared at the paused frame of the display case. “The emotional truth remains. Everyone left calmer. If the film treats that relief as gullibility, we exploit them a second time. The basement activity stopped.”

“We observed no further basement activity during the remaining visit,” Diane said. “That is the available statement.”

“You want a film about footnotes.”

“I want the dead left unnamed when the evidence cannot support a name.”

Miriam turned the ritual card over. On its blank side, she wrote one line and slid it across the table.

COMPASSION REQUIRES NO BIOGRAPHY.

Evan read it twice.

Abby opened the file from Diane’s auditorium recorder.

“Final item,” she said.

The waveform filled the center monitor. Abby identified ventilation, cloth movement, the piano key tapping beneath canvas, a distant seat spring, and breathing across most of the forty-five-second hold. At forty-one seconds, the input rose for seven-tenths of a second. Abby’s camera track contained a lower version of the same vent noise but lacked the brief central shape. Evan’s track contained a zipper movement near the same moment. Diane’s recorder had rested on the stage edge, several yards from the group.

“No one reported speech in the room,” Abby said. “The recorder microphone faced the auditorium. Automatic gain was active. The source may be local handling, acoustic reflection, equipment noise, or a voice below conscious audibility.”

She played the segment once.

A faint onset emerged under the vent. Three soft pulses followed. The last pulse ended in friction or breath.

Diane heard no stable words.

Evan leaned toward the speaker. “Marian.”

Miriam shook her head. “Not mine.”

Abby replayed it at the original level.

Evan changed his answer. “Maybe ‘one more time.’”

Miriam wrote on a slip before the third playback.

Diane watched the waveform rather than their faces. She could identify no clean consonants. When Abby raised the high frequencies, Diane heard the vent sharpen and the final friction approach an s. When Abby reduced them, Diane heard rounder middle pulses that sounded almost vocal.

Abby passed Diane the headphones.

Diane listened once at full speed. The headphones removed the computer fans and placed the vent modulation close against both ears.

Diane could divide the breath into four syllables if she followed one rhythm.

She listened again after Abby randomized it among five control clips from the same recorder. In one position she heard only vent noise; in another, the syllabic pattern returned. On the third pass, Diane could arrange the pulses into one possible four-word pattern: not my name.





She removed the headphones.

Evan watched her. “You heard it.”

“I heard one possible parsing.”

“What else?”

“Vent modulation. Fabric movement. A low voice. ‘One more time.’ ‘Not mine.’ Several combinations.”

Miriam opened her slip. She had written NOT MY NAME across the center.

Evan reached for the mouse. “That answers the whole night.”

Diane placed her hand over the controls.

“It answers nothing until a speaker and wording can be established.”

“It rejects Marian Bell.”

“It may contain no words.”

“It also rejects the student story. It rejects every name we gave it.”

“That interpretation repeats the same mistake in reverse.”

His hand remained near hers.

Abby locked the file and removed the working copy from the edit sequence. “The master stays under independent custody. No subtitle. No enhanced version in the documentary. We can state that unidentified audio appeared on one recorder and that listener interpretations differed.”

Evan looked at Miriam.

She folded her slip. “Use no name.”

The room stayed quiet except for the computer fans.

Diane opened a new page in the case log.

She authorized the ritual footage, the device sequence, the door event, the auditorium search, and the conflicting interviews. She required the chronology to remain intact. The black photograph could appear only with the handling gap. The meter recovery required the failed post-ritual press and battery reseating. The classroom material required removal of any real-student speculation. The auditorium segment required the visible program, rejected search matches, added search term, and lack of historical connection.

The final report could state that Miriam completed a bounded ritual, participants reported relief, and no further activity was observed during the remaining visit.

It could contain no claim that Bellweather High had been cleansed.

It could contain no named dead student, no identified auditorium woman, no attachment to Diane, and no declaration that any presence had crossed, remained, followed, protected, or departed.

Evan read the conditions on the monitor.

“You leave me almost no ending.”

Diane closed the facilities log. “You have the ending that occurred. We stopped provoking. We left together. We kept the recordings.”

Miriam placed her ritual card inside an envelope Abby provided. “That is enough for me.”

Evan looked from the card to the frozen waveform. He removed the proposed title card from the edit timeline. In its place, he typed:

SESSION COMPLETED. CAUSES UNRESOLVED.

Abby saved the project under a new version number.

Near noon, Miriam left through the side entrance. Evan carried the cameras to his car without filming Diane. Abby sealed the original cards and recorder copies in separate cases. She kept one set under the independent-custody agreement and signed the other into Diane’s facilities archive.

Diane returned to the basement office alone.

The same cabinet at the end of the workbench held the sealed wet brass key from the earlier case. Its evidence tag listed the recovery location, chain of custody, and source as unknown. She entered the shared combination, opened the upper door, and placed the auditorium recorder on the shelf beside it inside a separate padded case.

On the new tag she wrote:

AUDIO SOURCE UNIDENTIFIED.

NO OFFICIAL TRANSCRIPTION.

NO ESTABLISHED CONNECTION TO OTHER EVIDENCE.

She left space beneath the final line and added nothing.

The cabinet held two labels, two chains of custody, and no name.
